A||N||20||Bigfieroman||Bushings from Prothane don't fit?||||5||
Z||000000||Bigfieroman||07-31-2001||07:47 PM||||I asked my mechanic to install the poly cradle bushings in my car, but he hit a problem.  He says that the long and narrow bushings are the wrong diameter to fit.  he said that the diameter of the hole is 55mm and the area of the bushing that is supposed to fit in is only 52mm, and there is too much play.  I paid him an additional $100 to install them (over the $600 engine replacement charge) and he said he pressed the bushings out instead of burning them, so he was able to reinstall them.  Is he lying or are the bushings just defective?<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||07-31-2001||07:55 PM||||Yep. The front cradle bushngs are wrong. I had to mill them down so they would press into the steel sleeve the correct distance. They would have never fit without doing it.<P>The rears are fine.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-31-2001||08:28 PM||||If the hole is bigger than the bushing, Then he needs to get the sleeve off the rubber bushings. Usually the bushing is slightly bigger than the sleeve.<BR>From you discription the mechanic is trying to install the urethane bushings without the sleeve.||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||07-31-2001||10:44 PM||||Confusing string, First post states the prothane bushings were too small in diameter, Second post states prothane bushing had to be machined smaller to fit??<P>Can somebody clear this up for those of us who may be considering the Prothane Parts?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||08-01-2001||12:33 AM||||Sorry, more detail. The large lip that is outside the metal housing (on the flange side) was too long. If I had installed them, the bushing wouldn't have been centered in the metal housing correctly. There is no way they could have been installed unless you reversed one. In that case the front of the frame would have been shifted over.<P>As far as fitting in the metal housing itself, I milled out the old rubber and pressed the poly in. The fit was fine.<P>I had a set of front bushins sitting on the table with the poly and noticed that the poly was too long on that one side.||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||08-01-2001||12:46 AM||||terryk, thanks for the clarification!<P>Do you know if anyone has notified "The Fiero Store" or other business's that deal with Prothane about this condition, so they can correct it ???<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||watts||08-01-2001||01:59 AM||||What I'd like to know is had Prothane themselves been notified (I've had emails with before), and have they rectified this problem.  I bought a total set of bushings off of them but have yet to install them (missing some other bits and want to do it all at once).   <P>If there's going to be a problem with what I've got I'd rather know now!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">)<P>I've got an '87 - wonder if it makes a difference?  What year were you guys having trouble with?<P>Oh yeah - do you have accurate specs on the originals so I can measure what I have here?<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||California Kid||08-01-2001||10:32 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bigfieroman||08-01-2001||10:52 PM||||What area do I have to mill down?<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||08-02-2001||01:44 AM||||Don't start milling them down. You need to compare the poly bushings to the stock bushings. Your problem might be a different. <P>On the one's I received, the poly part wouldn't press into the sleeve far enough. It hit the flange too early. <P>I suggest you remove the factory bushing and compare first.<P>I wish I took pictures but I didn't. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan87GT||08-02-2001||05:32 AM||||I installed the Prothane cradle bushings this winter with no problems, I used a drill and ran it around the old bushings, wire brushed the left over rubber, left the new Prothane bushings in the freezer over night, covered them with the supplied grease and slipped them right in.....I ordered mine right from Prothane...a lot cheaper||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||08-02-2001||08:18 AM||||Dan... "you drilled throught them"?  Was that to allow some grease to get inside the bushing, to prevent squeeks?<P>Also, by freezing them, did they draw up?  If so, that is a wonderfull idea!<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||08-02-2001||12:09 PM||||If there is any motion in the bushings to cause noise, I'd be concerned.<P>Has anyone had their cradle bushings make noise?<P>Also, we did grease them with Vaseline and pressed them into the sleeves. The Vaseline disappears pretty fast. The end did mushroom out a bit from being squeezed into the sleeve, but nothing terrible. They aren;t coming out of there.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Bigfieroman||08-02-2001||04:41 PM||||I just called my mechanic and he said that he was trying to install the bushings without the sleeves.  Now he says that he is not sure that the bushings will fit even with the sleeves.  He says that even with the sleeves the bushings will be too small.  Is this true or is he mistaken?  He said that the hole and the bushings were 4mm different.  That means that the sleeves had to be 2mm thick.  Are they?  Please help me out, if anyone knows the thickness of the sleeves I would apprecciate it.  <P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||08-02-2001||09:19 PM||||I installed a set from Prothane about a year ago exactly,and all went well;I used the sleeve from the old bushings if there wasn't any provided.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Dan87GT||08-03-2001||07:51 AM||||When I say I drilled the old ones out, I mean I drilled a small hole between the metal sleeve and the end of the bushing, then I forced the drill sideways around the diameter of the old bushing to remove it. One another cradle I tried burning them out and the rubber material is nasty. I still find pieces around the house(in the carpet, and on my shoes). After freezing the Prothane bushings they did draw back and were tight..Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000016||bHooper||08-03-2001||09:54 AM||||Dan, I'm sorry, I missunderstood what you said.   That makes sense.  I have drilled, burned, and drilled and burned, followed by serious scraping.<P>I like the freezing thing and will keep that in mind for my next car.<P>As for the Prothane bushings, Bigfieroman, here's the deal... your mechanic sounds a little inept! Give him the following directions:<P>Have him salve the he!! out of the poly bushings, and install them in the <B>original</B> rubber bushing sleave[/b], then salve the inner sleave, and slip that into the bushing.  Lastly, he can instal the <B>original sleave w/poly bushing</B> back into position on the car.  <P>There is no way for this to be that confusing.<P>take part A from part B<BR>insert part C into part A<BR>insert part D into part C/A<BR>insert part D/C/A into part B<BR>reinstall part B into car.<P>Part A= Rubber Bushing/Sleave<BR>Part B= control arm or cradle<BR>Part C= Poly Bushing<BR>Part D= New Inner Sleave<P>or remove the rubbery black thing from the metal thnigy and replace with the red thingy, but dont forget the slippery stuff<P>plain and simple!<P>hope this helps, if not email me.<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||08-05-2001||01:22 PM||||Actually,,,, the sleeve needs to be in the cradle Before the polly goes in. You won't be able to press the sleeve w/o damaging the polly if you try it all at once.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Bigfieroman||08-07-2001||12:16 AM||||Thank you very much for the clarification Ogre.  I did the rears yesterday with no instructions (other than what I read here).  It took me just under 3 hours just to do the two rears because it took me 50 minutes just how to get the car jacked up without lifting the suspension or cradle.  First i had it on ramps, then I lifted it by the cradle, looked at it a while, and realized I had to take the wheel off, then finally found the small jack point that it meant to be used by the stock jack.  When I took it apart, I removed the rubber, and thought that I needed the steel insert that is bonded to the lower bushing.  I spent an additional 30 minutes pulling, cutting, and finally burning until I realized that I was retarded and wasting my time and didn't need the part.  First side took me 2 hours, and the second side took me about 35 minutes.  I am really proud of myself, because the next largest thing I have done to a car was changing the headlight on my GT.  I am going to attempt the fronts tomorrow or Wednesday using your technique outlined in another thread.  I was contemplating trying to remove the sleeves but you set me strait.  So, wish me luck and I will keep you all posted.  I always thought I had a little mechanic blood (or 10w40) running through these hands.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||08-07-2001||07:37 PM||||You left out which metal part?<P>the rear bushings do have a metal part that must be reused. The bushing should seperate from it rather easily.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Bigfieroman||08-07-2001||10:20 PM||||I used the easily seperated part...the part I am reffering to was molded into the rubber.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
